# Files consumed inside Linux containers (docker-compose / Dockerfile.ollama) or under WSL2
# must keep LF in the working tree regardless of core.autocrlf. A CRLF shebang makes the
# kernel look for interpreter "/bin/sh\r" and the container fails with "bad interpreter" -
# the classic "works on my machine, breaks on the freshly-cloned one" trap.
*.sh          text eol=lf
Dockerfile*   text eol=lf

# Generated sample-graph datasets (feature sample-graphs): built by
# fallen-8-web-ui/scripts/build-samples.ts, served from a public raw URL, imported verbatim
# as fallen8-jsonl. Mark generated so they stay out of diffs and language stats, and keep LF
# so the byte counts (and any meta-line guards) match what the build produced.
samples/*.jsonl              linguist-generated=true text eol=lf
fallen-8-web-ui/scripts/samples/data/fallen8-sbom.json linguist-generated=true

# Ingestable sample documents (feature knowledge-demo). The PDF must be marked binary
# EXPLICITLY: it happens to contain no NUL byte, so git's content heuristic classifies it as
# TEXT, and with core.autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows default) checkout rewrites its 138 LF
# bytes to CRLF. That shifts every offset after the first newline, so the trailer's startxref
# points into the middle of a compressed stream and the file is unreadable - and since
# `docker build` uses the working tree as its context, a Windows-built image would SHIP the
# broken PDF. The XLSX is a zip and does contain NULs, so git detects it correctly, but it is
# marked for the same reason: not by luck. The markdown one is genuinely text and is pinned to
# LF so the ingested bytes (and its content hash) are identical on every platform.
samples/documents/*.pdf      binary
samples/documents/*.xlsx     binary
samples/documents/*.md       text eol=lf
